WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950
Concurrency allows work to overlap; parallelism means work executes at the same

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.012
time on multiple processing resources.

00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:09.913
Shared mutable state requires coordination.

00:00:10.063 --> 00:00:14.060
A compound check followed by an update is not atomic merely because

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.725
individual field reads are safe.

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:22.509
Locks, atomic operations, immutable data, and message passing solve different coordination problems.

00:00:22.659 --> 00:00:27.581
Java 21 virtual threads reduce the cost of many blocking tasks, but

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:32.092
they do not create unlimited database connections, sockets, or provider quota.

00:00:32.242 --> 00:00:34.963
Bound access to scarce dependencies separately.

00:00:35.113 --> 00:00:40.334
The JVM manages heap allocation and garbage collection; memory leaks still occur

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:42.510
when objects remain reachable unnecessarily.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.610
Measure allocation, pauses, thread activity, and pool waits before tuning.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.345
InventoryTest launches twenty buyers using virtual threads.

00:00:51.495 --> 00:00:56.319
Database row locking, rather than the thread type, prevents overselling ten units.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:00.889
Replacing virtual threads with platform threads should preserve the business result.

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:05.542
The experiment separates concurrency implementation from correctness authority.

00:01:05.692 --> 00:01:10.135
Run 100 blocking read tasks with a semaphore allowing ten outbound calls.

00:01:10.285 --> 00:01:13.032
Record peak active calls and completion time.

00:01:13.182 --> 00:01:17.580
Add cancellation and ensure interrupted tasks restore or propagate interruption.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:22.479
The semaphore limits dependency pressure while virtual threads make waiting economical.

00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:26.596
A test should verify the peak active count, not only completion.

00:01:26.746 --> 00:01:30.965
Do not hold a database transaction while waiting for unrelated remote work.

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:31.786
Question.

00:01:31.936 --> 00:01:34.018
Do virtual threads prevent races?

00:01:34.168 --> 00:01:34.744
Answer.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:35.378
No.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:40.995
Shared-state correctness still requires appropriate synchronization or transactional coordination.

00:01:41.145 --> 00:01:41.816
Question.

00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:44.465
Why can a service be slow with low CPU?

00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:45.191
Answer.

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:50.519
It may be waiting on connections, locks, queues, disk, or downstream calls

00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:52.676
rather than executing CPU work.
